            <div xml:id="d808">

                <pb facs="0771" n="671" sameAs="frus:frus1920v03/pg671"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">763.72115/3731: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Wallace</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-05-14T23:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">May 14, 1920—11
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received May 14—9 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>1154. Mission. Your 917, May 12, noon. In response to my inquiry at Ambassadors
                    Conference this morning Laroche<anchor n="63" xml:id="d808-f1"/> stated that
                    French Government had expressed willingness to facilitate <pb facs="0772"
                        n="672" xml:id="pg672"/> transfer of Bela Kun and other Communists to Soviet
                    Russia provided Soviets permitted French citizens to leave Russia. Agreement
                    similar to British agreement with Soviets was being negotiated for repatriation
                    of French citizens held in Russia but Soviets were putting obstacles in the way.
                    He added that Hungarian Government might make objections. My British, Italian
                    and Japanese colleagues undertook to inquire of their respective Governments
                    when information received. Question will come up again in the conference.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wallace</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d808-f1" n="63" xml:id="d808-f1-content">
                <p>Jules Alfred Laroche, of the French Ministry of Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
